+## Swarm mode
+
+When **Settings → Swarm Mode** is on, the subtasks the plan allows to run side by side are built at the same time, each in its own worktree, and merged into the task's branch after each wave; a subtask in conflict goes back to the task's coder. Design: [swarm.md](https://github.com/krovomi/WorkPilot-AI/blob/develop/docs/architecture/swarm.md).
+
+## Execution waves (Mission Control)
+
+Mission Control groups the specs waiting in the backlog and the queue into waves that share no file, from their plans and from what their worktrees already changed, in any language (a dependency manifest or a lockfile weighs more). It shows the plan wave by wave, the merge order, the riskiest pairs, dependency cycles and a spec drifting outside its plan; **Run in the optimal order** starts each wave through the usual task start, each task with its own provider, within the project's parallel tasks, and replans the rest after each wave. No model is called. Design: [execution-waves.md](https://github.com/krovomi/WorkPilot-AI/blob/develop/docs/architecture/execution-waves.md).
+
+## Pair programming with an AI participant
+
+In a Pair Programming room, **Invite the AI** adds WorkPilot AI, which answers each message with the provider and model chosen for the page (every provider). It reads the conversation and the room's file, chosen in the open project; a code block it proposes shows as a suggestion on that file, never written. The room says it stays on this computer. On a server project, a team chat message that mentions `@ia` gets an AI reply, marked 🤖, computed by its author's app with the provider of their Insights page.
+
+## Sharing an agent replay
+
+A recorded session exports as a `.wpreplay` file a teammate imports into their WorkPilot (and compares with one of theirs), or as one HTML page anyone opens without installing anything. Secrets and local folders are always masked; reasoning, token counts and costs, and file contents leave only if you say so. Annotated bookmarks mark the steps of a finished session and travel with it. Design: [replay-sharing.md](https://github.com/krovomi/WorkPilot-AI/blob/develop/docs/architecture/replay-sharing.md).
+
+## Code migration
+
+The **Code migration** page (Toolbox) analyses a framework, version or language migration on any stack and shows the source stack, the target (the last stack named in the sentence), whether the plan is known, its risk, phases and steps. **Create the Kanban task** puts that plan in the task's description, and the task migrates in an isolated worktree. A stack that keeps its framework is planned by its languages (a React app moved from JavaScript to TypeScript, a Java service converted to Kotlin).
+
+![Code migration](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/krovomi/WorkPilot-AI/develop/.github/assets/screenshots/code-migration.png)
+
